      "parts": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "\"When using the Google Cloud Go client libraries, callers provide an auth.Token that may include a Type field indicating the authentication scheme (e.g., \\\"Bearer\\\", \\\"Basic\\\"). The documentation states that if the Type field is omitted (empty string), the token should be interpreted as a Bearer token. However, the current implementation concatenates the Type value directly, resulting in malformed Authorization headers when Type is empty. The required behavior is that any token lacking an explicit Type must automatically be treated as a Bearer token when constructing the gRPC request metadata. This defaulting should be applied consistently whenever the library builds the \\\"authorization\\\" entry for outgoing gRPC calls.\n\nThe solution must modify the code path that assembles the Authorization header for gRPC PerRPCCredentials. It should inspect the token's Type, substitute \\\"Bearer\\\" when the field is empty, and then combine the resulting type with the token's Value separated by a single space. The rest of the metadata map should remain unchanged, preserving any additional custom entries supplied by the caller. This change must be transparent to existing callers: tokens that already specify a non‑empty Type continue to be sent unchanged, while tokens with an empty Type now result in a correctly formed \\\"Bearer <value>\\\" header.\n\nAdditionally, the token provider interface should be able to return a token whose Type is empty without causing errors downstream. Tests should verify both scenarios: a token with an explicit Type (e.g., \\\"Basic\\\") produces \\\"Basic <value>\\\", and a token with no Type produces \\\"Bearer <value>\\\". The implementation should not rely on any specific internal helper names or constants; it only needs to enforce the defaulting logic and ensure the resulting metadata map contains the correct Authorization entry.\"\n",
          "id": "prt_ecd3182cb001LhNnZE6pWI0RkL",
          "sessionID": "ses_132ce7d4fffehibD3nXqywNAia",
          "messageID": "msg_ecd3182ca001kTemLj3jEm6RNc"
        }
      ]
